#### 2.2.3 Phenix

The Pioneering High Energy Nuclear Interaction experiment (PHENIX), is a compound detector primarily oriented to perform electromagnetic probes. The various sub-detector systems are able to capture hundreds of heavy ion events per second, 10 kHz [1]. The rare probes as a sign of the QGP formation, is the PHENIX physics goal. The apparatus consists of two large central arms, positioned symmetrically on equidistant sides, of the beam line.

As it can be seen in Figure 2.4, there are two muon forward spectrometers, covering \(1.1<|\eta|<2.2\). They consist of a muon tracker (consecutive layers of drift chambers) and a muon identifier (absorber made of steel and layers with streamer tubes [1, 2] of _Iarocci_ type [1]. PHENIX possesses two calorimeters in PbSc (Lead-Silicon) and PbGl (lead-glass) as can be seen on the left side of Figure 2.4. They serve to reconstruct the \(\pi^{0}\) and \(\eta\) neutral mesons. In the central arms, the particle identification is being performed where particles such as \((e^{\pm},\pi\) with \(p_{\mathrm{\tau}}\) up to 4 GeV/c) are detected using the Ring Imaging CHerenkov (RICH). For the \(\pi^{\pm},K^{\pm}\) up to \(p_{\mathrm{\tau}}=1.5\) GeV/c and the \(p^{\pm}\) up to 3.5 GeV/c the Time of Flight (TOF) serves for their identification [1, 2]. In addition, the muon arm serves to identify the of the charmed particle \(J/\psi\) and in particular its muonic decay \(J/\psi\to\mu^{+}\mu^{-}\).

