## Chapter 6 Results in Cu+Cu collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}=200\,\mathrm{GeV}\)

In the current chapter, we present the results obtained by performing the analysis on the Cu+Cu dataset at \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}=200\,\mathrm{GeV}\) and in particular the _High Tower_ trigger. The various cuts along with the invariant mass plots for each separate azimuthal \(\Delta\phi\) case, and the significance of the signal are presented as well.

### 6.1 Introduction

During the run V two different species (p+p and Cu+Cu at various energies as stated in Table 2.1) were used for collisions. Our analysis focuses on Cu+Cu at \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}=200\,\mathrm{GeV}\) and in particular the _High Tower_ triggered dataset. Concerning the STAR detector, let us also note that the Barrel Electromagnetic Calorimeter (BEMC) as it is presented in Section 2.16, is partially instrumented covering in acceptance \(\eta\in[0,1]\). The BEMC towers are accepted in the current analysis, only in the region \([0,0.7]\). The latter cut serves to avoid lots of material, close to the edges (\(\eta=1\)), crossing when the vertex is at \(z_{c}=\pm 30\,\mathrm{cm}\). In run V the overall dataset of Cu+Cu at \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}=200\,\mathrm{GeV}\) is distributed in the following data trigger setup names [20].

1. \(\sim\!30\cdot 10^{6}\) events in \(\mathrm{cuProductionMinBias}\); and
2. \(\sim\!17\cdot 10^{6}\) events in \(\mathrm{cuProductionHighTower}\).

The analysis is performed on the \(\mathrm{cuProductionHighTower\,data}\) subset.

### 6.2 Applied Event Cuts

#### Primary \(z\)-Vertex Cut

A primary vertex cut is performed on the \(z\) component, accepting only events that comply with \(|z\)-vertex\(|<30\) cm. In the current analysis 2795681 events were processed, passing--after the \(z\)-vertex cut--2191462, and finally accepting 26717 events, that contain a high-\(p_{\mathrm{r}}\) electron, as seen in Figure 6.1.

#### BEMC Energy Threshold

An online cut is imposed on the energy threshold of the BEMC tower at \(E>3.75\) GeV, complying with the values of Table 3.3, for the adequate trigger. The current trigger setup is used in order to obtain a high-\(p_{\mathrm{r}}\) triggered events, with a large probability of containing an \(e^{\pm}\) as the trigger particle. In addition for the electron identification the cuts described in Section 3.5 are applied.

### 6.3 Applied Track Cuts

#### Track Quality Cuts

The track selection, includes a cut on the \(|\eta|<1\) and the number of the points in the TPC. In particular nHitsTPC \(>20\) and \(\frac{\mathrm{nHitsFit}}{\mathrm{nHitsPos}}>0.15\), in agreement with (3.2). The total number of selected pairs per event, _samesign_ (\(++\) or \(--\)) and _unlike_ (\(+-\)) signcan be seen in Figure 6.2. Also in order to apply the microvertexing technique we are using the _global_ tracks as stated in Appendix E.

#### Particle Identification Cuts

In order to identify the hadron into different species, we select tracks that comply with the \(\frac{dE}{dx}\) cut: as described in Section 3.6 and in particular for the whole \(p_{\tau}\) spectrum, respecting (6.1).

\[|\mathrm{n}\,\mathrm{Sigma}\mathrm{Kaon}|<2\ \mathrm{and}\ |\mathrm{n}\, \mathrm{Sigma}\mathrm{P}\mathrm{ion}|<3 \tag{6.1}\]

A supplementary cut on the kaon candidates is also imposed involving the charge. In particular if the charge of the hadron is equal to the electron charge, then the hadron becomes a kaon candidate, as described in Section 1.13. In this way, we are looking for contribution mainly from the \(c\bar{c}\) at \(\Delta\phi=180^{\circ}\) and \(b\bar{b}\) at \(\Delta\phi=0^{\circ}\) fragmentation channel as it is summarized in Table 1.2.

