## Chapter Results in Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}=200\,{\rm GeV}\)

In the current chapter the description of the results obtained performing the analysis on the Au+Au dataset at \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}=200\,{\rm GeV}\). The analysis is also performed on the _Btag_ as well as the Minimum Bias triggered events. We present the various cuts along with the invariant mass plots for each azimuthal \(\Delta\phi\) correlation case.

### 8.1 Introduction

During the run VII the overall dataset of the Au+Au at \(\sqrt{s_{{}_{\rm NN}}}=200\,{\rm GeV}\) dataset, is distributed in the following trigger setup names [Didenko 08].

1. Approximately \(62\cdot 10^{6}\) events in the 2007ProductionMinBias, (including only the _physics_ datastream); and
2. \(\sim 27.9\cdot 10^{6}\) events in the 2007Production2, all the datastreams.

In particular, in the trigger setup name 2007Production2, the subset of events with the _Btag_ stream is the portion of events that the \(e\)-\(D^{0}\) analysis will be focused on. The high-\(p_{\rm r}\) events belonging to this stream are \(\sim 1.5\cdot 10^{6}\) in total. Let us also mention that for the Minimum biased events--in the _physics_ stream and in the 2007Production2--are approximately \(18\cdot 10^{6}\) events are included.

### 8.2 Applied Event Cuts

#### Primary \(z\)-Vertex Cut

A primary vertex cut at \(|z\)-vertex\(|<20\,{\rm cm}\) is performed, allowing to select tracks, that remain restrained in the STAR central tracking area. In total 1.461.003 events areexamined (belonging in the _Btag_ stream and the 2007Production2). After the above primary \(z\)-vertex cut, 1415616 events are gathered. Finally the portion of the 93727 events used for the current analysis are obtained that contain a high-\(p_{\mathrm{r}}\) electron, as can be seen in Figure 8.1.

#### Quality of Runs

It was suggested by the Physics Working Group of STAR Collaboration, that a certain amount of runs obtained should be excluded from the analysis [10]. In particular, the criterion for the selection of runs it is examined the \(\langle\cos\phi\rangle\) of tracks versus the run-number is plotted for several \(p_{\mathrm{r}}\) bins (with a bin size of 50 MeV/c and done up to \(p_{\mathrm{r}}=2\) GeV/c). The request is whether each run has a mean value of the \(\langle\cos\phi\rangle\) greater than \(2\sigma\) from the overall mean value described by (8.1).

\[\begin{split}\langle\cos\phi_{c}\rangle&=-0.006\\ \sigma_{\cos\phi}&=0.003\end{split} \tag{8.1}\]

As a result, the _Btag_ sample is affected by 7.46% yielding 6847 rejected events. For the minimum biased events in 2007Production2, this demand affects 200 runs (or equivalently 675.294 events), or 3.82% of the total events.

#### BEMC Energy Threshold

The online cut implemented on the transverse energy \(E_{\mathrm{r}}\) of the BEMC tower threshold is at \(E_{\mathrm{r}}>4.75\) GeV, and complies with the energy threshold values stated in Table 3.3, for the adequate triggers. A tower is accepted only if it is found within the pseudorapidity area \(\eta\in[-0.7,0.7]\). The latter cut is performed in order to avoid the edges of the calorimeter where the material becomes thick, when compared to the \(\eta=0\) region.

### 8.3 Applied Track Cuts

#### Track Quality Cuts

The quality assurance of tracks is performed by demanding for each track to have the TPC hits that comply with (3.2). Also tracks outside the region \(|\eta|<1\) are excluded from the analysis. After the track cuts, the total number of selected pairs per event, both for the _samesign_ (\(++\) or \(--\)) as well as for the _unlike-sign_ (\(+-\)) can be seen in Figure 8.2. Also in order to perform the microvertexing technique we are using the _global_ tracks as stated in Appendix E.

#### Particle Identification Cuts

The classification of hadrons into \(K\) and \(\pi\) is performed by selecting tracks that comply with the \(\frac{dE}{dx}\) cut as described in Section 3.6. The values chosen for the current dataset, are described in (8.2).

\[|\mathrm{n}\,\mathrm{Sigma}\mathrm{Kaon}|<2\ \mathrm{and}\ |\mathrm{n}\, \mathrm{Sigma}\mathrm{P}\mathrm{P}\mathrm{on}|<3 \tag{8.2}\]

Additionally, a supplementary selection on the kaon candidates is also imposed. In particular the demand of the charge of the hadron to be equal to the electron charge, yields a kaon candidate, that can probe a \(D^{0}\) produced by a \(c\bar{c}\) at \(\Delta\phi=180^{\circ}\) or a \(b\bar{b}\) at \(\Delta\phi=0^{\circ}\). In the current dataset, we also implemented the unlike sign charge demand between the trigger particle and the hadron, that combined with the adequate \(\Delta\phi\), it can probe from a \(b\bar{b}\) fragmentation channel. The latter charge correlations for the \(c\bar{c}\) and \(b\bar{b}\) along with the azimuthal correlations, are described in Section 1.13.

