### A.4 Energy in the Center of Mass for Fixed Target and Collider Experiments

Let \(E_{1}\) be the energy of the beam of particles \(m_{1}\) that hit particles of mass \(m_{2}\) with \(E_{2}\). The energy at the CMS can be calculated using the square of the 4 momenta, as expressed below, in the laboratory frame.

\[p^{\mu}p_{\mu} = E_{\mathrm{{}^{\mathrm{T}or}}}^{2}-\vec{p}_{\mathrm{{}^{\mathrm{ T}or}}}^{2}\] (A.10) \[= (E_{1}+E_{2})^{2}-(\vec{p}_{1}+\vec{p}_{2})^{2}\] \[= p_{1}^{2}+m_{1}^{2}+p_{2}^{2}+m_{2}^{2}+2E_{1}E_{2}-(p_{1}^{2}+p _{2}^{2}+2\vec{p}_{1}\cdot\vec{p}_{2})\] \[= m_{1}^{2}+m_{2}^{2}+2(E_{1}E_{2}-\vec{p}_{1}\cdot\vec{p}_{2})\]

In the center of mass the invariant mass is given by (A.11).

\[p^{\mu}p_{\mu}=\tilde{E}_{\mathrm{{}^{\mathrm{T}or}}}^{2}-\tilde{\vec{p}}_{ \mathrm{{}^{\mathrm{T}or}}}^{2}\] (A.11)

Since in the CMS the total momentum is \(|\vec{p}_{\mathrm{{}^{\mathrm{T}or}}}|=0\) then (A.11), is transformed into (A.12).

\[p^{\mu}p_{\mu}=\tilde{E}_{\mathrm{{}^{\mathrm{T}or}}}^{2}\] (A.12)

Since the quantity \(p^{\mu}p_{\mu}\) is invariant, relations (A.12) and (A.10) are equal, yielding:

\[\tilde{E}_{\mathrm{{}^{\mathrm{T}or}}}^{2}=m_{1}^{2}+m_{2}^{2}+2(E_{1}E_{2}- \vec{p}_{1}\cdot\vec{p}_{2})\] (A.13)

Furthermore we distinguish the two separate cases: for fixed target described in Section A.4.1 and collider as described in Section A.4.2.

#### Fixed Target

For the fixed target experiment, we can consider that \(|\vec{p}_{2}|=0\) yielding \(E_{2}=m_{2}\). Therefore, (A.13) is transformed into (A.14).

\[\tilde{E}_{\mathrm{{}^{\mathrm{T}or}}}=\sqrt{m_{1}^{2}+m_{2}^{2}+2E_{1}m_{2}}\] (A.14)In the ultra-relativistic regime where \(E_{1}\gg m_{1},m_{2}\) then the available energy (A.14), in the center of mass (CMS) is a function of the square root of the colliding energy beam (\(\sqrt{E_{1}}\)) for a given target mass \(m_{2}\).

#### Collider

In the case of a collider, there are two anti parallel beams of \((E_{1},p_{1})\) and \((E_{2},p_{2})\), with \(\vec{p_{1}}\uparrow\downarrow\vec{p_{2}}\). Therefore (A.13), becomes (A.15).

\[\tilde{E}_{\mbox{\tiny{T\,or}}}=\sqrt{2(E_{1}E_{2}+|\vec{p_{1}}||\vec{p_{2}}| )+m_{1}^{2}+m_{2}^{2}}\] (A.15)

In the ultra-relativistic limit \(p\to E\) or equivalently \(m\to 0\), yields (A.16).

\[\tilde{E}_{\mbox{\tiny{T\,or}}}=\sqrt{4E_{1}E_{2}}\] (A.16)

Finally in the case of equal energy beams (\(E_{1}=E_{2}=E\)), the available energy \(\tilde{E}_{\mbox{\tiny{T\,or}}}\) is only a function of the \(2E\). As a graphical representation, Figure A.1 depicts both cases (fixed target and collider) concerning the available energy at the CMS \(\sqrt{s}\) as a function of the \(E\). In Table A.1 the values of the energies are shown both for the collisional as well as fixed target experiments used in heavy-ion physics.

