## Appendix C Statistics

### The Uniform Distribution

Let us consider the case where the variable \(x\) is equally distributed, among the interval \([a,b]\), following the _uniform_ distribution. The probability density function \(f(x)\) is given by (C.1).

\[f(x)=\left\{\begin{array}{ll}\mathcal{K},&\quad a\leq x\leq b\\ 0,&\quad\text{otherwise}\end{array}\right.\] (C.1)

let be \(\mathcal{K}\) a constant. The normalization of the function \(f(x)\) imposes that

\[\int_{a}^{b}dxf(x)=1\] (C.2)

yielding

\[\mathcal{K}=\frac{1}{b-a}\] (C.3)

The mean value is given by (C.4)

\[\langle x\rangle\equiv\int_{a}^{b}dx\,xf(x)\] (C.4)

or equivalently

\[\langle x\rangle=\mathcal{K}\frac{x^{2}}{2}\bigg{|}_{a}^{b}=\frac{1}{b-a} \frac{b^{2}-a^{2}}{2}=\frac{b+a}{2}\] (C.5)

In order to calculate the _variance_ given by (C.6)

\[\sigma^{2}=\langle x^{2}\rangle-\langle x\rangle^{2}\] (C.6)first we have to calculate the quantity

\[\langle x^{2}\rangle=\int_{a}^{b}dx\,x^{2}f(x)\] (C.7)

where

\[\langle x^{2}\rangle=\left.{\cal K}\frac{x^{3}}{3}\right|_{a}^{b}=\frac{1}{b-a} \frac{b^{3}-a^{3}}{3}=\frac{1}{3}(a^{2}+ab+b^{2})\] (C.8)

The variance then becomes:

\[\sigma^{2}=\frac{1}{3}(a^{2}+ab+b^{2})-\frac{(b+a)^{2}}{4}=\frac{1}{12}(a-b)^{2}\] (C.9)

Finally accepting the non negative roots of (C.9), the _standard deviation_ is given by (C.10).

\[\sigma=\frac{|a-b|}{\sqrt{12}}\] (C.10)

For the case where \(a=0,\ b=1,\)\(\langle x\rangle=0.5\) and \(\sigma=\frac{1}{\sqrt{12}}\) as it is graphically depicted in Figure C.1.

Figure C.1: Constant distribution of pseudo-random number within the interval \([0,+1].\) The mean value is \(\langle x\rangle=0.5\) and the standard deviation \(\sigma=\frac{1}{12}\simeq 0.288.\)

